@* Despite being a .cshtml extension and having HTML content, we use the @asset
   directive to select the default parser and not HtmlEncode content. That would
   be a bad idea in actual practice but this is for testing. *@
@asset "NotHtml.txt"
@{
    string wouldNeedEncoding = "'\"'";
    string something = "something";
    string name = "Phred";
    string one = "1";
    string two = "2";
    string three = "3";
}

This is not HTML.

But this looks like an HTML tag that would get special processing if this were HTML
<p class="@something">

This also looks like an html tag but is not. The attribute value should not be encoded.
<div attr="@wouldNeedEncoding">Something</p>

And here is something that might be interpreted as a tag but it might actually source code
if x <p q="@one"

And since it is left dangling then an HTML reader might treat it as if all of this
sentence is still part of the tag.

Name = @name

Just to keep things interesting. Here is a syntax-correct HTML element but in quotes
as if some program were running.
"<strong>Some strong text</strong>"
<strong r="@name">Some strong text</strong>
